  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the average of outcomes that are probable (i.e., with probability > 1/n, where n is the number of outcomes). In this case, Lottery A has one probable outcome (-4000 Euros), and Lottery B has none. The reason would compare the average of probable outcomes, but since B has no such outcomes, the comparison is undefined. Even if we assume that a lottery with no probable outcomes is worse, the reason would favor A. However, the individual did not consider this rule. Instead, they focused on the presence of a 0 Euro outcome (which is not probable in A) and the subjective insignificance of the loss difference. The individual did not mention probabilities above 0.5, nor did they discuss averaging outcomes based on a threshold. Their justification was based on avoiding loss and subjective perception of loss magnitude, not on the probabilistic structure of probable outcomes. Therefore, the information considered by the individual (existence of 0 Euro outcome, subjective loss difference) does not align with the information required by the decision reason (probable outcomes based on threshold). Furthermore, the justification does not reflect the rule of preferring the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes. Thus, the individual did not use the decision reason to make the decision.",
